<Legal
    title="Privacy policy"
    description="What Sikemux collects, why, who handles it, and how to have it deleted."
    updated="3 October 2026">
    <p>
        Sikemux is made by nodelike. This page covers the Sikemux app for your computer, the Sikemux phone app, your
        Sikemux account at <a href="https://app.sikemux.com">app.sikemux.com</a>, and this website. Questions go to <a href="mailto:contact@nodelike.com">contact@nodelike.com</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Your code, files, terminals, agent chats, prompts and dictation stay on your devices. We never receive them.
        </li>
        <li>
            Connections between your devices are encrypted end to end. A relay server that passes them along cannot read
            them.
        </li>
        <li>
            A Sikemux account holds your email address and the list of devices signed in to it. It exists so your
            devices can find each other.
        </li>
        <li>We do not sell data, show ads, or share data with anyone except the services listed below.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your Sikemux account</h2>
    <p>
        The phone app needs an account. The app on your computer works without one and asks you to sign in only to be
        found by your phone. When you have an account, we keep:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Who you are:</strong> your email address, and the name and profile picture Google or GitHub share if
            you sign in with them. The Sikemux apps show them so you can see which account you are signed in to. If you choose a password, only a hash of it is kept, never the password itself.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your devices:</strong> for each device signed in to your account, its name (such as your computer's
            name or your phone's model), its platform, which Sikemux release it runs, the public key it identifies itself
            with, and when it was added and last updated.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A record of account changes:</strong> when a device was added to or removed from your account, so you
            and we can see what happened to it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Sign-in sessions:</strong> which browsers and devices are signed in, with the IP address and browser
            details they signed in from, to keep your account secure.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Signing out of the phone app removes that phone from your account. Signing out on your computer stops new
        devices finding it through the account.
    </p>

    <h2>Server logs</h2>
    <p>
        Our servers log each request they handle: the address asked for, the answer, how long it took, and which account
        made it. The web server also logs the IP address and browser of each visit. We use these logs to keep the service
        running and to investigate abuse, and they are deleted automatically as newer logs replace them.
    </p>

    <h2>Usage statistics</h2>
    <p>
        Official releases of the Sikemux app for your computer send one message a day while you use them. It contains a
        random ID made for your install, the app version, its update channel, and your operating system, its version and
        your processor type. It contains nothing about you or your work, and we use it to count how many people use
        each version. PostHog, which receives it, sees the IP address it comes from, as any server does. You can turn it off with Share anonymous usage, under Privacy in Settings, About. Builds you make
        yourself never send it.
    </p>
    <p>
        This website counts page views and clicks on the Download button without cookies and without identifying you.
    </p>

    <h2>What the apps download</h2>
    <p>
        The app checks GitHub for updates and downloads them from there, along with the profile pictures of the
        people credited in its release notes. When you open a file in a language it has not shown before, it downloads that language's
        highlighting rules from jsDelivr or unpkg. If you use dictation, it downloads the speech
        model from Hugging Face once, and then turns speech into text on your computer. Those sites see your IP address
        when they serve the download, as any website does.
    </p>
    <p>
        Each time it opens, the phone app asks our server at updates.sikemux.com whether a newer version of its own code
        is available, and downloads it from there. Every update is signed, and the phone checks the signature before
        using it. With each check the app sends its platform, an ID for the app build it is, and its update channel, so
        the server can pick the right update. Our server logs these requests like any other, as described above.
    </p>

    <h2>Coding agents</h2>
    <p>
        Sikemux runs agents such as Claude Code and Codex on your computer. Each agent talks to its own company under its
        own terms and privacy policy, and Sikemux does not send what you or your agents write to us.
    </p>

    <h2>Who handles data for us</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Clerk</strong> runs sign-in and stores your account details.</li>
        <li><strong>Google and GitHub</strong> confirm who you are when you choose to sign in with them.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> checks that a new account is being made by a person, not a bot.</li>
        <li><strong>Hostinger</strong> hosts the server that keeps your device list and serves phone app updates.</li>
        <li><strong>PostHog</strong>, in the EU, counts the usage statistics and website visits described above.</li>
        <li><strong>Vercel</strong> hosts this website, and <strong>GitHub</strong> hosts the downloads.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>n0</strong>, the makers of the networking library Sikemux uses, run the relay servers that pass
            encrypted connections between your devices when they cannot reach each other directly. When remote access is
            on, your computer also lists its public key and its relay with n0's lookup service, so your devices can find
            it.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Keeping and deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
        We keep your account and device list for as long as you have an account. To delete your account, email <a href="mailto:contact@nodelike.com">contact@nodelike.com</a> from the address on it. We delete the account
        and its devices within 30 days and confirm when it is done. The steps are on <a href="/delete-account">Delete your account</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        We back up the account database so it can be restored if something goes wrong. Backups are kept for 30 days and
        then deleted, so a deleted account is gone from them within 30 days too.
    </p>
    <p>
        You can also ask for a copy of what we hold about you, or for anything wrong to be corrected.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Sikemux is not meant for children under 13, and we do not knowingly keep accounts for them.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
        If this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it. If a change affects what we collect, we will say so
        in the app or on this website before it takes effect.
    </p>
</Legal>
